```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when `kobject_init_and_add()` fails, it takes a reference to the kobject, but the original code incorrectly uses `kfree(type);` to free the memory. This does not release the reference taken by `kobject_init_and_add()`, leading to a reference count leak since the kobject remains allocated but is not properly released.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = kobject_init_and_add(&type->kobj, &mdev_type_ktype, NULL,
                           "%s-%s", dev_driver_string(parent->dev),
                           group->name);
if (ret) {
    kobject_put(&type->kobj);
}
```
Reason:
The fix replaces `kfree(type);` with `kobject_put(&type->kobj);`, which correctly decrements the reference count of the kobject when `kobject_init_and_add()` fails. This ensures that the memory associated with the kobject is properly cleaned up, preventing the reference count leak.

